  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is consistently zero (0.00), the model exhibits a severe upward bias, forecasting significantly higher prices than actual outcomes; correct by applying a negative adjustment factor of approximately -15% to the forecasted value.",
    "The model fails to capture mean reversion during high-volatility periods, instead projecting a steady linear trend that ignores sharp downward spikes in true outcomes when recent history shows extreme volatility.",
    "When sentiment shifts from zero to positive (e.g., 0.40 or higher), the model's upward bias diminishes but remains insufficient; apply a dynamic correction that increases as sentiment rises, reducing the negative adjustment factor by roughly 50% for every 0.20 increase in sentiment.",
    "The model systematically underestimates the magnitude of price drops following a peak, assuming smooth continuation rather than the erratic 'spike and drop' pattern observed in true outcomes during low-sentiment regimes."
  ]
}
